@* Why the model field is free text. A per-response note, not a stored one: it describes
   the probe this response is the answer to, and the saved state already carries the
   lasting truth in its badge. A plain page load therefore shows the picker with no note
   rather than repeating a warning about a probe the user never asked for.

   The wording is load-bearing and it was wrong twice. It used to say the connection is
   "saved and usable either way", which is false for a base URL that already carried a
   version segment: the probe asks for {base}/v1/models, so a base ending in /v1 asks for
   /v1/v1/models, comes back 404, and the completion would post to
   /v1/v1/chat/completions and 404 too — the connection is saved, and enriching with it
   fails. The replacement then over-corrected to "enrichment will fail on any of the
   three", which is false for the FIRST of them: a service that does not serve a model
   list still answers /v1/chat/completions, and that working case is the entire reason
   this state exists (the e2e stub is one, live, in the test that pins this sentence).
   So the three causes are named and the note stops there. What is true of all three is
   that they are indistinguishable from here, so the note is a statement about
   diagnosability and says nothing at all about what enrichment will do — in either
   direction. Whether the app should normalise a typed /v1 instead is a design question
   the register carries as an open entry, not something this copy decides.
   It also must not claim the opposite ("it is not a sign of a bad key"), because a
   rejected key produces this exact badge. The e2e pins the clauses that name the causes
   the old wording denied and the clause that says they cannot be told apart, so the
   revert is red, and it pins the absence of any enrichment claim, so a future edit that
   puts one back in either direction is red too. *@
@model string
<div class="alert alert-warning" role="alert">
    <strong>Could not confirm the connection.</strong>
    Your service did not answer the model-list test, so the connection is saved but
    unconfirmed. The usual reason is an endpoint that does not offer a model list — but a base
    URL your service does not serve, and a key it rejected, look exactly the same from here,
    so the app cannot tell you which of the three it was. Name the model yourself below.
</div>
